# NAME: Binance Direct
# FORMAT: Surge RULE-SET (policy omitted)
# SOURCE: forced-DIRECT measurements from the home line, 2026-09-18
# MIRROR: Graham-lo/surge
# UPDATED: 2026-09-18
# NOTE: Binance endpoints that are reachable from mainland China without a proxy,
# so they should bypass it. Reference this set with the DIRECT policy immediately
# before Binance.list.
#
# 2026-09-18 revision. The August list forced the *.binance.com websocket hosts
# DIRECT, but that path is dead: fstream/stream/dstream/nbstream/ws-api
# .binance.com now get a poisoned A record domestically and are reset during the
# TLS handshake even when dialled on their real IPs, so every entry below was
# re-measured under a forced-DIRECT rule before being kept or added. Blocking is
# per hostname, not per domain: fstream.binance.me connects while
# fapi.binance.me does not, so nothing here may be widened to a DOMAIN-SUFFIX.

# Futures market push. 101 Switching Protocols over DIRECT with live production
# frames, TLS handshake ~0.30s. These carry the real book, not testnet.
DOMAIN,stream.binancefuture.com
DOMAIN,dstream.binancefuture.com
DOMAIN,fstream.binancefuture.com
DOMAIN,fstream.binance.me

# Spot public data mirrors. Push and REST both answer over DIRECT
# (/api/v3/klines returns 200 in ~0.33s); data.binance.vision serves the
# historical archives.
DOMAIN,data-stream.binance.vision
DOMAIN,data-api.binance.vision
DOMAIN,data.binance.vision

# Static asset CDN on China Unicom Guangdong. Answers in 30-40ms over DIRECT.
DOMAIN,static.bscdnweb.com
DOMAIN,public.bscdnweb.com

# Domestic acceleration addresses used by the Binance iPhone app itself. All 25
# still accept TCP 443 over DIRECT and present *.bsappapi.cc or a Binance-owned
# certificate, so they stay. They only fire when a Binance app host resolves onto
# them; our own app never dials these.
IP-CIDR,39.108.55.122/32,no-resolve
IP-CIDR,39.108.92.184/32,no-resolve
IP-CIDR,39.108.179.41/32,no-resolve
IP-CIDR,39.108.226.125/32,no-resolve
IP-CIDR,39.108.239.197/32,no-resolve
